---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/recart-alternative';
const title = 'Recart alternatives for Shopify: replacement decisions';
const desc = 'Compare the work behind managed SMS, capture and cart recovery before choosing a replacement.';
const faqs = [{"question": "Must I leave Recart to improve recovery?", "answer": "No. First trace capture, permission, cart events and purchase exits with your current operator. Repair a demonstrated gap or test a replacement against that same job."}, {"question": "Can I move every exported phone number?", "answer": "An export is not sufficient acceptance evidence. Reconcile sender, purpose, channel, disclosure and current withdrawals; hold incomplete records out of activation."}, {"question": "Does a cheaper SMS quote prove savings?", "answer": "No. Match message segments, destinations, service labor, fees and discounts, then distinguish attributed orders from incremental contribution."}, {"question": "When is cutover ready?", "answer": "When capture, queue cancellation, purchase exits, withdrawals and retry deduplication pass and export, contract and sender arrangements are confirmed. There is no universal duration."}];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Recart alternatives for Shopify</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl">Compare the work behind managed SMS, capture and cart recovery before choosing a replacement.</p>
    <div class="prose-shopify mt-12">
      <h2>Keep the service if it solves the staffing problem</h2>
      <p>Recart is a managed SMS option, not simply a generalist bundle. Its <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/recart">current Shopify listing</a> describes a dedicated success manager, desktop/mobile popups, welcome/cart/reorder automation and costs shown before sending. Those are useful benefits for a merchant who needs operating help. They do not establish incremental profit or guarantee that every account receives the same service.</p>
      <p>Ask your current operator to trace one acquisition source through permission, welcome, checkout recovery and purchase exit. Staying and repairing a documented failure can cost less work than rebuilding capture and creative elsewhere. Choose a replacement when you can identify the missing behavior, its owner and the resources to run it.</p>
      <h2>Choose a replacement around the missing job</h2>
      <p>For a sender change, ask an SMS candidate to demonstrate your checkout event, destination coverage, inbox and suppression requirements. For capture, require the mobile direct-to-text and desktop paths separately; a basic popup does not prove equivalent behavior. For managed work, quote strategy, creative, approvals and reporting separately from software. Do not interpret an email integration as a replacement for the SMS service.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="/alternatives/attentive-alternative">Attentive alternative guide</a> addresses a broader email/SMS journey handover. Investigate that scope if you also intend to consolidate channels. A separate email tool, including Sequenzy, needs its own Shopify event and permission acceptance exercise; adding one is not a prerequisite for leaving Recart.</p>
      <h2>Proposed cart acceptance job: a footwear store</h2>
      <p>This is a proposed exercise, not a measured merchant result. Use a test shopper who selects a size, opts into the stated SMS purpose and abandons checkout. Record cart ID, variant ID, event time, source disclosure/version, sender, channel and permission timestamp. Verify the destination stores usable evidence, not just a subscribed flag. Keep missing or contradictory records out of activation; email permission is not SMS permission.</p>
      <p>Queue recovery, then purchase before dispatch. Expect cancellation for the converted cart. Repeat with an out-of-stock size, changed price, expired discount and an old checkout link. The acceptable result is either valid current merchandise and offer or no recovery send. Check a second active cart independently rather than suppressing or replaying every cart by phone alone.</p>
      <p>Retry the same event after destination acceptance and simulate an acknowledgement timeout. Reconcile accepted message IDs before retrying an unknown outcome; event/cart/purpose identity must prevent duplicate recovery. Recheck permission, purchases, stock, price and quiet hours at dispatch, including after a quiet-hour delay. A delayed purchase event must still stop queued recovery when current order state shows conversion.</p>
      <h2>Capture and handover gates</h2>
      <p>Test mobile and desktop forms with existing subscribers, a declined checkbox and a withdrawal arriving after export. Check that the welcome offer is not issued twice and that a new form does not revive an opted-out record. Inventory active keywords, embedded forms, scripts, discounts, integration credentials and destinations before replacing any collection surface.</p>
      <p>Name one operator for reply handling, STOP/HELP tests and rejected imports. Verify both the sending system and connected support workflow receive current withdrawals. Reconcile source/export/import counts by accepted, rejected, duplicate and suppressed state. Set frequency and customer-timezone rules to the actual promise and operating policy rather than an arbitrary universal cap.</p>
      <p>At cutover, pause old triggers, cancel their queued sends and record the last accepted event/message IDs. Give one system ownership per recovery purpose. If acceptance fails, pause the new queue and reconcile all accepted sends and later withdrawals/purchases before resuming eligible work in the old system. Do not blindly replay an export or restore a stale subscription snapshot. Confirm export access, contract notice and any sender transfer with both providers before uninstalling.</p>
      <h2>Keep margin and relevance in the decision</h2>
      <p>For an illustrative $100 basket with $45 goods and $10 other variable costs, contribution is $45 before marketing. A $25 discount leaves $20 before messaging. Twenty incremental orders at $20 less $300 campaign cost leave $100; thirty less $450 leave $150. These are assumptions, not supplier prices or observed recovery results. Discounting alone does not prove a loss.</p>
      <p>Quote the same ordinary and peak workload: destinations, segment count, SMS/MMS, carrier and number charges, platform fees, creative/service labor and discounts. Compare opt-outs by capture source and flow, not just total list growth. Click-attributed orders are not necessarily incremental; use a suitable holdout where feasible and disclose uncertainty.</p>
      <p>Keep opted-in VIP launch access separate from discount audiences. For replenishment, use product usage evidence and exclude gift buyers when the purchaser is not the user. Coordinate educational email and urgent SMS with explicit offer ownership and current purchase exits; do not manufacture a fixed collision window.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
